Z||000003||GodSend||09-09-2000||01:13 PM||||  Variety is the spice of life my brother - When I have an idea I try to persue it instead of just going with the status quo - I wanted to see what a nice proper chrome job would look like - Add a little extra pazzaz , thats all.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.hypernetcommunications.com/revn.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||09-09-2000||01:36 PM||||Well, if you want to get an idea what it would look like, apply "Kentucky chrome," a.k.a. silver metallic paint to the grilles.  That way, if you don't like it, you can go back to black.<P>To answer your question, no, I haven't ever seen chrome grills on a Fiero.  (Since you didn't ask for my opinion, I won't say what I'm thinking right now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> )  It <B>is</B> your car, so you can do what you want with it.  I suspect yours <B>would</B> be the only one, if you went with chromed grills.<BR>   <BR>Here's another thought - what if you painted the grilles same color as the body of the car?  Just a thought - might make it unique, too. <P><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 09-09-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000005||Phil||09-09-2000||06:41 PM||||I've seen the vents polished to look like chrome (in my opinion not good).||||reg||1
Z||000006||myblack86GT||09-09-2000||11:15 PM||||A friend of mine has an 87GT, Silver, and has crome engine grilles. I like the idea, but it doesent look that good on his silver car IMO. I do think they would look good on a black or red car. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Homicidal||09-09-2000||11:30 PM||||Depends on the color of the car mostly, and if anything else is chrome.<P>I mean, a Red GT with Gold rims and CHROME engine grates would look horrible.<P>But a Black SE with Chrome rims might look nice.<P>Just put tinfoil over the grates and stand back!||||reg||1
Z||000008||GodSend||09-10-2000||03:18 PM||||  Well, the only thing that would look good is real chrome - no fakes.  You can see the color of my car in my sig.  And that paint is mucho expensive - so It would cost me a few bucks to paint thje grills that color.<P>  My car also has silver rims - so I think it would look pretty good - I also try not to give my personal opinions on all the hood scoops, deck scoops, and side scoops I see on some cars - If you ask me thats very rice as well , but to each his own.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.hypernetcommunications.com/revn.gif"> ||||reg||6
Z||000009||JohnnyK||09-10-2000||03:21 PM||||The paint on my grills is horrible looking, so I was thinking of sandblasting them to get that dull, but silver look. What do you guys think?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||09-10-2000||03:54 PM||||I just bought a set of '85 engine covers (solid style) for the Grape Ape.  After I get Toms decklid scoop, I'll paint these to match the car and install them.  I like that monochromatic look.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000011||StuGood||09-11-2000||12:12 PM||||I think I hear opportunity knocking...<P>JohnnyK:  If the black is flaking off your grilles, you might try hand sanding the accessible areas first, towards getting the "dull silver look."  If that appeals to you so far, next sandblast "gently" (don't want to pit the surfaces) to turn whole thing silver.  Install it on your car, take a  picture, and post it for GodSend's benefit (and for the rest of us).<P>Even if you decide silver's not for you, the sanding /blasting wasn't a wasted effort, because now you'll have a nice clean surface to prime and paint.<P>Monkeyman:  You might want to run a little test to see how a small swatch of your "monochromatic look" paint holds up to heat/ battery vapors, before you paint the whole thing.<P>Just a few thoughts (and a lot of words - sorry about that).   <BR>        ||||reg||10
Z||000012||Monkeyman||09-11-2000||07:24 PM||||Monochromatic=all one color (like the '88s).  I have pearlized purple paint now (hence the name <I>Grape Ape</I>).  It covers everything but the vents.  It's obviously an older paint job and hasn't loosened because of the heat so far.  I don't think I'll have any problems, but thanx for the warning. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||webbee||09-11-2000||08:01 PM||||On soft metal, glass bead is much better than sand. Stripper might work too, if there isn't any plastic involved.||||reg||1
